<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Locarno Treaties</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Locarno Pact</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno">Locarno</a> Treaties were agreements negotiated at Locarno in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in October 1925 and signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> that December. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> accepted their common borders, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as guarantors, and Germany agreed arbitration with its eastern neighbours. The treaties eased post-war tensions and led to Germany's entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/locarno-treaties.jpg" alt="Locarno Treaties" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R03618,_Locarno,_Gustav_Stresemann,_Chamberlain,_Briand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under the Treaty of Versailles Germany lost 13 per cent of its European territory and 12 per cent of its population, chiefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and a restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and its territory west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> was occupied by Allied troops. Revising the treaty became an aim of German politicians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>. The foreign minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-stresemann">Gustav Stresemann</a>, hoped to win the goodwill of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> by fulfilling its terms, and wanted peace with France, the recovery of land lost to Poland and an end to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and the occupation. France was chiefly concerned with security and had signed treaties with Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p><p>The push came when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> postponed their withdrawal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> region after finding violations of the disarmament provisions. In early 1925 Stresemann sent secret memorandums to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> proposing a treaty by which the parties interested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> borders would settle their issues peacefully. Opposition at home was strong, especially to renouncing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The delegates met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno">Locarno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, from 5 to 16 October 1925, and there were seven agreements. In the main treaty of mutual guarantee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> guaranteed the inviolability of the German-Belgian and German-French borders fixed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and promised to observe the demilitarised zone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. Germany and France, and Germany and Belgium, promised not to go to war with each other, with three exceptions, and to settle disputes peacefully with the League of Nations Council involved.</p><p>Arbitration agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and arbitration treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, set up procedures for settling disputes. The treaties with Poland and Czechoslovakia gave no guarantee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s eastern borders. France also signed treaties of mutual assistance with Poland and Czechoslovakia. The treaties were formally signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 1 December 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> approved the treaties in November 1925 by 291 votes to 174, which led to the fall of the Luther government. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> they were seen as a defeat, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a> said that every honest Pole spits when he hears the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno">Locarno</a>. British troops left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> region in January 1926, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> with a permanent seat on the Council on 10 September 1926. The last occupying troops left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> in 1930. Austen Chamberlain received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1925, and Aristide Briand and Stresemann shared it in 1926.</p><p>Contemporaries spoke of the spirit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno">Locarno</a>, but Hans Mommsen wrote that it concealed a tenacious struggle over national interests. The treaties went out of force in practice on 7 March 1936, when German troops entered the demilitarised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and the other signatories responded only with verbal condemnation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Locarno Treaties” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Foreign Minister Gustav Stresemann sought revision by peaceful means: the <b>Locarno Treaties</b> of 1925 settled the western borders, Germany joined the League of Nations in 1926, the Treaty of Berlin kept relations with the Soviet Union open, and in 1928 he helped mediate the Kellogg–Briand Pact.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
